[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Server-Side Rendering (SSR) and Nuxt.js Architecture in Vue.js"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Server-Side Rendering (SSR) is a technique that renders client-side single-page applications (SPAs) on the server rather than in the browser. Nuxt.js is a powerful framework built on top of Vue.js that simplifies the creation of SSR applications, offering automatic code splitting, great SEO performance, and a structured architecture."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding SSR vs Client-Side Rendering"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a traditional Client-Side Rendered Vue application, the server sends a minimal HTML file with empty root elements and a bundle of JavaScript. The browser then downloads, parses, and executes the script to render the page content. With SSR, the Nuxt server executes the Vue components, renders the full HTML markup on the server, and sends it directly to the client, improving initial load times and Search Engine Optimization."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Nuxt.js Architecture Overview"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Nuxt.js enforces a strict file-system routing convention and modular architecture. Key directories include:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"pages/: Automatically generates Vue Router routes based on the file structure."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"components/: Houses reusable Vue components."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"server/: Contains Nitro server routes, middleware, and API endpoints."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"composables/: Auto-imported composition functions for state and logic."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating an SSR Data Fetching Example"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Nuxt 3, data fetching on the server is typically handled using the 'useFetch' or 'useAsyncData' composables. These hooks ensure data is fetched during the server-side render phase and seamlessly passed down to the client."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"<script setup>
const { data: posts, error } = await useFetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts');
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    <h1>Server-Rendered Blog Posts</h1>
    <div v-if="error">
      <p>Failed to load posts.</p>
    </div>
    <ul v-else>
      <li v-for="post in posts" :key="post.id">
        <h2>{{ post.title }}</h2>
        <p>{{ post.body }}</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</template>"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Lifecycle Hooks in SSR"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building Nuxt.js applications, you must be mindful of code execution environments. Lifecycle hooks like 'onMounted' only run on the client side. If you attempt to access browser-specific APIs such as 'window' or 'document' inside setup scripts without guards, it will cause hydration mismatches or server crashes."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"<script setup>
import { onMounted, ref } from 'vue';

const windowWidth = ref(0);

onMounted(() => {
  windowWidth.value = window.innerWidth;
  window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
    windowWidth.value = window.innerWidth;
  });
});
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    <p>Current Window Width: {{ windowWidth }}px</p>
  </div>
</template>"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Nuxt.js bridges the gap between high-performance server-rendered applications and the reactive developer experience of Vue.js. By understanding its directory structure, utilization of data fetching composables, and lifecycle execution contexts, you can build scalable, SEO-friendly applications with ease."}]
